WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:13.133
Good evening and a very warm welcome
to the Royal Library.

00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:14.833
My name's Lise Back Hansen -

00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:19.400
- and my colleagues and I are really happy
to see so many here tonight -

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.467
- for the International Authors' Stage
with Karl Ove Knausgård.

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:28.400
Also a warm welcome to the audience
watching our live stream -

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.133
- both to you at home
and all of you gathered at the libraries.

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:38.700
It's really great that we get together
to celebrate literature.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.467
And it really is a celebration, actually.

00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:46.967
This event is created in collaboration -

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:52.133
- with the Hans Christian Andersen
Award Prize Committee.

00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:59.200
Every two years, this committee awards
a major international literature prize -

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:04.300
- which Karl Ove Knausgård
received in 2022.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.100
And the awards committee
motivated the prize like this:

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.133
"He receives it for his intense renewal -

00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:17.567
- of the field between
novel and autobiography -

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:22.700
- a field which Hans Christian Andersen
also explored -

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.667
- and for,
just like Hans Christian Andersen -

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:31.467
- positioning Scandinavia
on the literary world map."

00:01:31.567 --> 00:01:36.300
Karl Ove Knausgård made his debut in 1998.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:43.433
His works are translated into 36 languages
and are published all over the world.

00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:48.500
But it is Knausgård's giant project
'My Struggle' -

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.600
- which he published in a series
of six autobiographical books -

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.533
- that established his name
internationally.

00:01:57.633 --> 00:02:02.833
Recently, the novel series 'The Morning
Star', 'The Wolves of Eternity' -

00:02:02.933 --> 00:02:05.867
- and most recently, 'Det tredje riket' -

00:02:05.967 --> 00:02:09.133
- were all published in Danish
by Lindhardt and Ringhof.

00:02:09.233 --> 00:02:15.533
We plan to talk about it all here tonight,
and I'm certainly looking forward to that.

00:02:15.633 --> 00:02:19.733
For this purpose,
we've asked Kathrine Tschemerinsky -

00:02:19.833 --> 00:02:26.167
- to interview Karl Ove Knausgård
about his work here on stage.

00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:30.300
She has written about his writing
for a number of years -

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.933
- and she has read him
since he began writing.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:39.867
And additionally, she's also
the culture editor at Weekendavisen.

00:02:39.967 --> 00:02:44.567
In addition, the actor Benjamin Kitter
is also on stage.

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:48.800
He's here because we want to hear excerpts
from the works -

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:54.867
- and we want to hear it in the beautiful
Danish translation by Sara Koch.

00:02:54.967 --> 00:03:00.233
Finally, I want to extend my warm thanks -

00:03:00.333 --> 00:03:02.500
- to our excellent collaborators -

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.700
- the trade union DM
and the unemployment fund MA -

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:14.033
- that enable us to offer tickets cheaply
to students in our Students Only network.

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:19.367
I also want to praise the collaboration
with the H.C. Andersen prize committee -

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:23.700
- and of course, to Lindhardt and Ringhof.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.300
An evening like this is only made possible
thanks to the great collaboration -

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.500
- within Danish culture.
Thank you so much.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:38.267
So please welcome Karl Ove Knausgård,
Kathrine Tschemerinsky and Benjamin Kitter.

00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:04.767
- Are you comfortable?
- I'm good.

00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:07.600
We'll be here for a while.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:11.467
Some years ago, quite a few years ago,
you wrote an essay -

00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:17.733
- claiming that 'Madame Bovary'
was the best novel ever written.

00:04:17.833 --> 00:04:19.900
Is that still your opinion?

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.333
It changes from week to week -

00:04:23.433 --> 00:04:26.200
- but it's up there among the top ten.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.600
Why is it that good?

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.267
It...

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.600
I'm trying to recapitulate and bring it
back. It's a long time since I read it.

00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:57.333
It has an incredible visual precision.

00:04:57.433 --> 00:05:00.567
A realistic precision.

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:08.500
And in all that precision,
it has something very imprecise -

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:15.233
- which is about a kind of desire...

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.267
Do you want to add something?

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.467
- Perhaps.
- We can return to it.

00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:41.667
The reason I started by asking you
about this is -

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:46.133
- that tonight we'll talk about the novel
and first and foremost -

00:05:46.233 --> 00:05:50.100
- we'll talk about how the novel
has taken shape in your work.

00:05:52.333 --> 00:05:58.533
Do you remember the first time you became
aware there was something called a novel -

00:05:58.633 --> 00:06:03.533
- and that it was different
from other forms of literature?

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.133
I remember...

00:06:11.233 --> 00:06:16.267
I remember when I started reading novels -

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:23.133
- and I remember
how obsessed I was with it.

00:06:23.233 --> 00:06:30.900
And I remember the first book
that made a difference.

00:06:32.367 --> 00:06:39.800
It was a book by Ursula K Le Guin
called 'A Wizard of Earthsea' -

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:44.333
- that I received as a present
when I was ten.

00:06:44.433 --> 00:06:47.533
I thought it was amazing,
and I read it again and again.

00:06:47.633 --> 00:06:53.933
I've actually read it again as a grownup,
and I still think it's very good.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:02.467
And I couldn't reflect on
why I thought it was important.

00:07:02.567 --> 00:07:07.300
There was a kind of total involvement
and a total identification.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:12.700
Among other things,
it's about haughtiness and transgression -

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:15.000
- but also about boundaries
between life and death.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:21.167
And it's visualized and describes well
the transition into the land of the dead -

00:07:21.267 --> 00:07:24.600
- and something from there
enters our world.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:30.000
The protagonist,
who is young and haughty and ambitious -

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:34.467
- and is intelligent and gifted -

00:07:34.567 --> 00:07:37.433
- wants to be a wizard.

00:07:37.533 --> 00:07:40.667
This is before Harry Potter.
Many years before Harry Potter.

00:07:40.767 --> 00:07:44.533
He attends a sort of wizard school,
but he goes too far.

00:07:44.633 --> 00:07:49.333
He can do things that others can't and lets
something into the world that stalks him.

00:07:49.433 --> 00:07:52.833
He then has to find the name
of whatever is pursuing him -

00:07:52.933 --> 00:07:55.867
- that is driving him really far.

00:07:55.967 --> 00:07:59.367
At the end of the book,
which for a ten-year-old was amazing -

00:07:59.467 --> 00:08:02.800
- he sniffs at this creature
that threatens him -

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:04.900
- and then he sees his own name.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.533
- Wow.
- That was it.

00:08:07.633 --> 00:08:11.233
That meant...

00:08:11.333 --> 00:08:15.700
Can you use a phrase like existentially
significant about a ten-year-old?

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:17.733
But that's what it was.

00:08:17.833 --> 00:08:21.300
What was it about that book
that meant so much?

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:23.133
- About it?
- Yes.

00:08:23.233 --> 00:08:28.100
There were these strong forces
in a person -

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:32.300
- that I didn't understand -

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.567
- but that I identified with
and experienced.

00:08:39.733 --> 00:08:45.333
We'll now see a letter on the screen.
I told you about it the other day.

00:08:45.433 --> 00:08:47.500
You asked me why do you send me this.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:53.233
It's a letter from Hans Christian Andersen
that he sent to Charles Dickens in 1857 -

00:08:53.333 --> 00:08:57.000
- after having spent five incredibly
long weeks with the Dickens family -

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:01.300
- and Dickens had him up to here.
He writes to thank him.

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:03.600
It ends with something I'll just read out -

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:07.300
- because I don't think anyone
can read the writing.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:08.733
He says:

00:09:08.833 --> 00:09:15.133
In my mind, I organize a new novel where
the humorous element will be prominent.

00:09:15.233 --> 00:09:19.567
Of course, it takes place in our time.
I don't know any other.

00:09:19.667 --> 00:09:21.800
It stays in Denmark,
there are my roots.

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:24.067
Live hearty well!

00:09:24.167 --> 00:09:29.233
You asked me why I thought of you
when I stumbled upon that letter.

00:09:29.333 --> 00:09:35.667
Normally when you talk
about writers and their novels -

00:09:35.767 --> 00:09:41.300
- you imagine it's something
they've visualized way out on the horizon:

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:45.333
"I want to write this novel,"
and then they write that novel.

00:09:45.433 --> 00:09:49.000
But what I think is so nice
about this letter from Andersen is -

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:52.700
- that in a way he is setting up
his own space of opportunity.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:58.800
He recognizes that he can't write
like anyone about anything.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:03.600
It made me want to ask you
what kind of author you can be -

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:05.833
- or have realized that you can be.

00:10:05.933 --> 00:10:08.867
That I can be or want to be or am?

00:10:08.967 --> 00:10:12.333
That you must be,
because you can't write any other way.

00:10:12.433 --> 00:10:17.333
- What did you say?
- Because you can't write any other way.

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:32.200
For a long time, I couldn't write.

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.800
To begin with, I could write.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:37.300
Writing was easy. I wrote lots.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:42.033
It was incredibly immature and quite bad,
but it was just writing.

00:10:42.133 --> 00:10:47.933
And then I got in touch
with the best literature.

00:10:48.033 --> 00:10:51.700
I got into a writing academy.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:58.800
Then suddenly I couldn't write anymore,
I saw what was good and what wasn't.

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:01.500
It was as if...

00:11:03.933 --> 00:11:10.333
You may see in a lab,
they have an object inside a box -

00:11:10.433 --> 00:11:14.933
- and stick their hands into gloves
and move things around. That's how I wrote.

00:11:15.033 --> 00:11:18.600
It was quite distant
and quite rigid and quite mechanical.

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:20.367
And then...

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:28.067
... suddenly when I was 26,
I suddenly could write.

00:11:28.167 --> 00:11:31.600
There were many reasons for that, however -

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:38.067
- what happened was
that writing became like reading.

00:11:38.167 --> 00:11:41.400
So writing was the same as reading
when I grew up.

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:43.833
It was completely different.

00:11:43.933 --> 00:11:47.533
It was something
I couldn't have thought out.

00:11:47.633 --> 00:11:50.133
It was as if literature took over.

00:11:50.233 --> 00:11:51.867
But it was very realistic.

00:11:51.967 --> 00:11:54.367
It was very much based
on my own geography -

00:11:54.467 --> 00:11:58.633
- a little my own family,
a little my own father.

00:11:58.733 --> 00:12:01.500
Everything was there -

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:06.167
- a little changed,
but it was quite a realistic world.

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:12.700
I was in love with myself
because I could write.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:18.267
At the end of it, a passage appears
that is fantastic, not realistic, fantastic.

00:12:18.367 --> 00:12:24.333
It's about a world that doesn't exist.
People walk around with lynxes...

00:12:24.433 --> 00:12:27.533
A fantastic universe.

00:12:27.633 --> 00:12:30.433
When I wrote it, I thought -

00:12:30.533 --> 00:12:33.333
- there was something there
that was really important for me.

00:12:33.433 --> 00:12:38.833
My editor, Geir Gulliksen, said,
"We need to remove this. It doesn't fit in."

00:12:38.933 --> 00:12:41.800
I said, "I want it in."

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:45.133
Then he wrote me a letter
and sent it by mail.

00:12:45.233 --> 00:12:47.833
He's never done that,
neither before nor since -

00:12:47.933 --> 00:12:51.600
- and said,
"Dear Karl Ove, can't we remove it?"

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:53.900
And I said, "No."

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.100
I always listen to him and do what he says,
but I said no.

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:03.333
The reason I said no
was that it was my first book -

00:13:03.433 --> 00:13:07.333
- and this was an opportunity
I had in my writing.

00:13:07.433 --> 00:13:09.367
I didn't want to be only a realistic author.

00:13:09.467 --> 00:13:12.633
I wanted to be an author
who could write the fantastic.

00:13:12.733 --> 00:13:15.633
Because I grew up
with the fantastic, right?

00:13:15.733 --> 00:13:20.033
When I tried to become a writer, I tried
to write like Gabriel Garcia Marquez -

00:13:20.133 --> 00:13:24.767
- or like Salman Rushdie
or one of the fantastic authors of the 80s.

00:13:24.867 --> 00:13:28.667
I wanted both parts,
what is close and intimate -

00:13:28.767 --> 00:13:34.500
- and that which is not, which is fantastic.
Which to me is literature.

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:37.100
That's why I was so happy
with my second book.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:41.400
I thought, "Now I'm an author."
Because it's about angels that don't exist.

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:45.033
It's about something that doesn't exist.
But, they couldn't just be angels.

00:13:45.133 --> 00:13:49.067
They had to exist in a specific place,
so they were placed in Norwegian nature -

00:13:49.167 --> 00:13:52.667
- so it's the Bible in Norway.
It's well-known, but it's also literature.

00:13:52.767 --> 00:13:57.767
There's a kind of duality that I think
is present in everything I've written.

00:13:57.867 --> 00:14:03.733
After the point
where you find a way to write -

00:14:03.833 --> 00:14:07.500
- do you have the feeling that you develop -

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:11.333
- in addition, of course,
to writing new books, I mean as an author?

00:14:11.433 --> 00:14:13.433
Did you find a form?

00:14:13.533 --> 00:14:18.067
Is it like a sweater you put on one day -

00:14:18.167 --> 00:14:21.000
- and then you think,
"Wow, this sweater feels nice."

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:23.933
"Now I want to wear this
for the rest of my life." Or how is it?

00:14:24.033 --> 00:14:27.533
It's as if...

00:14:31.133 --> 00:14:34.100
If you write... or if you read a book -

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:39.467
- you want to read something that you could
not have foreseen, that you don't know -

00:14:39.567 --> 00:14:42.900
- because if you know what's going
to happen, there's no reason to read it.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.100
It's just like that when you write.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:49.133
You sit down, there must be something
you don't know.

00:14:49.233 --> 00:14:52.933
There must be something
that's interesting for you.

00:14:55.333 --> 00:15:00.133
And when I'd written my debut book -

00:15:00.233 --> 00:15:02.933
- it took five years before the next book,
and that's why.

00:15:03.033 --> 00:15:06.000
It would just have been a continuation,
but I'd finished it.

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.733
I had to find a new place.

00:15:08.833 --> 00:15:12.433
After the second book,
another five years passed. Same thing.

00:15:12.533 --> 00:15:15.967
I really tried to write every day,
went to work, tried to write -

00:15:16.067 --> 00:15:19.067
- it didn't work
because everything was known.

00:15:19.167 --> 00:15:22.800
You need something unknown.
Then I started on 'My Struggle'.

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:28.433
You'd think that would be quite familiar.
You write about yourself. On the contrary.

00:15:28.533 --> 00:15:32.467
And there I found a language
I hadn't used before -

00:15:32.567 --> 00:15:38.167
- and it lasted six books.

00:15:38.267 --> 00:15:44.733
I could've continued with 'My Struggle 7',
'My Struggle 8', 'My Struggle 9', but why?

00:15:44.833 --> 00:15:49.133
There was nothing left there
that would be new.

00:15:49.233 --> 00:15:53.233
Then I started writing short texts,
and I always wanted to do that.

00:15:53.333 --> 00:15:58.233
When I grew up and started reading in the
90s when I wanted to become an author -

00:15:58.333 --> 00:16:02.867
- minimalism and short texts
were important.

00:16:02.967 --> 00:16:08.100
I read 'Punch' that wrote short texts
about things.

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:11.633
I thought it was excellent.
There were no people, only things.

00:16:11.733 --> 00:16:15.233
But it was vibrant and incredibly good.
I wanted to write like that.

00:16:16.233 --> 00:16:20.733
I sat down and made a list of maybe
200 things that I wanted to write about.

00:16:20.833 --> 00:16:24.667
"I'll write about them."
There was nothing to write about.

00:16:24.767 --> 00:16:28.767
After 'My Struggle',
I'd learned to lower the threshold -

00:16:28.867 --> 00:16:30.833
- so that I'd accept everything that comes -

00:16:30.933 --> 00:16:34.167
- so I sat down with a list of words
and then wrote short texts.

00:16:34.267 --> 00:16:37.467
And it was 20 years later
or 15 years later.

00:16:37.567 --> 00:16:42.067
And it's entirely different
from writing long autobiographically.

00:16:42.167 --> 00:16:46.233
Even though the thoughts are the same,
and the way of writing is the same.

00:16:46.333 --> 00:16:52.133
Just the form makes what you think
different in the text.

00:16:52.233 --> 00:16:57.400
After that, I started on 'The Morning Star'.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:17:02.233
It seems you always foresee that you must
change course because of your framework.

00:17:02.333 --> 00:17:06.533
The short texts were a seasonal quartet -

00:17:06.633 --> 00:17:11.267
- so right from the beginning,
you prepared for the end of the quartet.

00:17:11.367 --> 00:17:14.500
The series you're working on now
with 'The Morning Star'...

00:17:14.600 --> 00:17:17.667
I asked you how many volumes
you expect, and you said six.

00:17:17.767 --> 00:17:20.800
Great! We can look forward
to three more books.

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:25.133
But you've established a framework:
It will end at some point.

00:17:25.233 --> 00:17:29.933
You can't just keep writing
about the morning star forever.

00:17:30.033 --> 00:17:36.467
Why? Is it then part of this idea
that you know you'll tire of it?

00:17:42.167 --> 00:17:44.800
What you want is...

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:49.900
Ingmar Bergman,
the Swedish director, said -

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.667
- that the goal of a scene
was for it to create a new scene -

00:17:53.767 --> 00:17:57.633
- that will create a new scene
and so forth.

00:17:57.733 --> 00:18:02.600
You embark on something,
which is the creative process itself.

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:04.533
And that's what I want.

00:18:04.633 --> 00:18:10.700
And so I have to set a framework or
a rule or... Not that it's very deliberate.

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:15.667
But, for example, in these books,
there are many different characters -

00:18:15.767 --> 00:18:20.200
- that then give a very specific direction,
a specific way of thinking -

00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:23.200
- and specific possibilities.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:28.133
Actually, I had planned one book. It then
became three and then five and then six.

00:18:28.233 --> 00:18:33.233
That's because the possibilities open up
as a result of that starting point.

00:18:33.333 --> 00:18:38.267
You said before, when I asked how you found
out which author you could be -

00:18:38.367 --> 00:18:41.767
- you said,
"Do you mean the ones I'd want to be?"

00:18:41.867 --> 00:18:46.000
In essays you've written that you completely
stay away from certain authors -

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:48.133
- because they're so good -

00:18:48.233 --> 00:18:52.200
- that being confronted with them
is unbearable.

00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:55.200
Do you still feel that way
about certain authors -

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:59.633
- that you simply don't read them
because they're too good?

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:06.300
No, I've become more courageous,
so now I dare.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:09.233
But the feeling is the same.

00:19:11.133 --> 00:19:16.833
It's quite difficult, right?

00:19:18.233 --> 00:19:23.867
But... by and large I read what I want.

00:19:23.967 --> 00:19:26.900
Why have you become more courageous,
do you think?

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.800
- Why?
- Yes.

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:32.967
I think...

00:19:37.867 --> 00:19:42.800
Yes, you talk about what kind of author
one wants to be. Okay.

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:47.600
Think about Hans Christian Andersen.
What kind of author was he?

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:50.800
He was...

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:53.333
He was full of energy -

00:19:53.433 --> 00:19:58.333
- and he didn't seem to have
self-criticism.

00:19:58.433 --> 00:20:02.167
It just came, right?
It just streamed through him.

00:20:02.267 --> 00:20:05.200
He has left all kinds of things.

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:08.533
But there's not one work where you think,
"That's Hans Christian Andersen."

00:20:08.633 --> 00:20:12.567
"We'll forget all the rest."
It's the whole package.

00:20:12.667 --> 00:20:18.300
And if you look at his contemporary,
Søren Kierkegaard, it's the same thing.

00:20:18.400 --> 00:20:22.767
 There's an enormous energy
in all directions during a short period -

00:20:22.867 --> 00:20:25.067
- when all his known works came out.

00:20:25.167 --> 00:20:29.567
It's the same thing: You don't think
of one work but the whole package.

00:20:29.667 --> 00:20:32.667
Think of August Strindberg in Sweden.
Same thing.

00:20:32.767 --> 00:20:37.933
My ideal, and I'm not there,
is to write books, publish them -

00:20:38.033 --> 00:20:40.900
- write the next one, publish it...

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:44.400
It's the very process, being in it,
that is the point.

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:48.933
If you think the authorship goes up there,
down there, in there, out there.

00:20:49.033 --> 00:20:53.967
Then you have to let go of the idea
that everything should be terribly good.

00:20:54.067 --> 00:20:57.833
That's cowardly, but I say,
"I don't have to write good books."

00:20:57.933 --> 00:21:02.733
It's a way of guarding myself, if people say
it's bad, I say, "Yes. Never mind."

00:21:02.833 --> 00:21:08.133
But it's also a way to manage
to write many books: to lower the threshold.

00:21:08.233 --> 00:21:14.233
What comes here, I accept,
and then I write on.

00:21:14.333 --> 00:21:19.767
Because when I couldn't
write for five years...

00:21:19.867 --> 00:21:24.633
There were 800 pages of beginnings
with first pages or first sentences.

00:21:24.733 --> 00:21:26.900
It never moved on.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:33.933
That's what I've learned:
That's the kind of author I want to be.

00:21:34.033 --> 00:21:37.033
The one who just writes.

00:21:37.133 --> 00:21:41.233
Some months ago,
you gave a fabulous speech.

00:21:41.333 --> 00:21:44.133
'Why the Novel Matters" was the title.

00:21:44.233 --> 00:21:47.100
It was so weird listening to it.

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:50.533
It was later printed,
so you can find it on the web.

00:21:50.633 --> 00:21:54.400
But it was so weird listening to it
because it suddenly dawned on me -

00:21:54.500 --> 00:22:00.300
- how inspired you've been by the
high-modernist writers, Joyce and Woolf.

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:01.700
It was weird.

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:08.833
Like having friends that suddenly tell you
they're really into baking.

00:22:08.933 --> 00:22:12.433
And you think, "What! I never knew."

00:22:12.533 --> 00:22:17.100
And you realize afterward that they always
served you cakes they'd just baked.

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:22.800
But it made so much sense
that you were into those authors -

00:22:22.900 --> 00:22:25.333
- and that they meant something to you.

00:22:25.433 --> 00:22:30.000
But could you talk about that time
and those writers?

00:22:30.100 --> 00:22:32.633
What have you taken with you from them?

00:22:32.733 --> 00:22:39.867
Yes... that's interesting.
Because you read books.

00:22:39.967 --> 00:22:44.933
And I never remember what I've read -

00:22:45.033 --> 00:22:47.033
- but I read -

00:22:47.133 --> 00:22:49.833
- and I write.

00:22:49.933 --> 00:22:57.633
Then... back to that story again
when I couldn't write and hadn't debuted -

00:22:57.733 --> 00:22:59.767
- I read Marcel Proust.

00:22:59.867 --> 00:23:03.067
Discovered Marcel Proust. I was obsessed.
I read him all summer.

00:23:03.167 --> 00:23:06.600
I had no money. I was drinking water
in cafes reading Marcel Proust.

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:11.267
Absolutely excellent.
Two years later, I debuted as an author.

00:23:11.367 --> 00:23:15.633
I didn't see a correlation, but now I see
that I have taken everything from there.

00:23:15.733 --> 00:23:19.800
The whole way of thinking literature,
the way of thinking metaphors.

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:25.233
For he is one who opens up all the time,
right?

00:23:25.333 --> 00:23:28.600
When I started writing,
minimalism was the thing.

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:32.033
If it was bad, you had to delete the bad.

00:23:32.133 --> 00:23:34.800
The idea was that what was left
should be really good.

00:23:34.900 --> 00:23:37.267
But with Proust it's the opposite.

00:23:37.367 --> 00:23:42.033
He adds and adds and adds
and expands.

00:23:42.133 --> 00:23:47.600
He might have a scene.
A scene like this with people.

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:51.833
He may make this look
like something under water -

00:23:51.933 --> 00:23:57.800
- with anemones and things like that.
There's a light somewhere.

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:02.433
With just one metaphor,
he's established two worlds, one here -

00:24:02.533 --> 00:24:07.433
- and an underwater world, and then
you can be in that underwater world.

00:24:07.533 --> 00:24:12.500
You can open world after world after world.
They're all next to each other.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:16.667
With a metaphor you can lift it in
and open room after room.

00:24:16.767 --> 00:24:20.533
It's totally contrary to minimalism, right?

00:24:22.233 --> 00:24:25.800
But I wasn't aware that
that's where it came from. But it was.

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:30.300
I also read Joyce -

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:34.833
- when I studied literature
in Bergen in the 90s.

00:24:36.933 --> 00:24:40.200
I was 20 and don't think
I understood a lot of it.

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:45.800
It's not that it's difficult
but it's unusual.

00:24:47.867 --> 00:24:54.433
I've returned to that book time and again.
I've read it multiple times.

00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:04.467
And because it takes place on one day -

00:25:04.567 --> 00:25:05.967
- it's like...

00:25:07.067 --> 00:25:10.900
There's a whole world,
a whole universe -

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:14.133
- that plays out on that day,
but it doesn't have access to the story -

00:25:14.233 --> 00:25:19.633
- only to the story that takes place right
now. There's really no superstructure.

00:25:19.733 --> 00:25:22.267
Everything is just
splinters and fragments around -

00:25:22.367 --> 00:25:25.700
- just like we live our lives.

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:31.100
Superstructures and ideologies don't exist.
It's always here and now, right?

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:34.033
That's a fundamental insight from that book.

00:25:34.133 --> 00:25:39.767
When I wrote that essay, it's about
that these books, such as Joyce -

00:25:39.867 --> 00:25:43.000
- enter the world
before it has become history -

00:25:43.100 --> 00:25:45.933
- before it's been thought,
"This is the way it is."

00:25:46.033 --> 00:25:48.967
It enters
while it's still coming into being.

00:25:49.067 --> 00:25:52.933
Just as I have no idea what
you're going to say in one minute.

00:25:53.033 --> 00:25:58.000
No clue. I don't know, right? It will
happen, but I don't know what it'll be.

00:25:58.100 --> 00:26:04.100
That's where Joyce enters. Right there.
And that's where we live and exist.

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:07.200
And oddly,
Virginia Woolf does the same thing.

00:26:07.300 --> 00:26:10.400
That's why they're so good:
they're about nothing.

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:15.600
There's Bloom having breakfast,
going to the bathroom -

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:17.967
- going out into the streets,
thinking a little.

00:26:18.067 --> 00:26:22.133
Nothing happens,
but that's what life is.

00:26:22.233 --> 00:26:28.633
And through the novel,
he manages to get in there.

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:35.533
In the same essay, I use another example -

00:26:35.633 --> 00:26:40.967
- which is a book that's very topical now,
it's from Ukraine -

00:26:41.067 --> 00:26:46.600
- at around 2014-15 in the Donbass area -

00:26:46.700 --> 00:26:50.567
- before the war escalated,
but there was still a war then.

00:26:50.667 --> 00:26:54.067
We've read lots
of journalistic reportage from there.

00:26:54.167 --> 00:26:57.867
On-the-spot journalistic reportage:
"This is what it's like, this happens."

00:26:57.967 --> 00:27:02.067
"This happens. We are in control.
We know how it is."

00:27:02.167 --> 00:27:05.100
He goes in and is there.

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:07.700
Nothing's happened yet.
There is nothing.

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:12.300
Some may be enemies, not enemies.
Some speak Russian, some speak Ukrainian.

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:18.000
Don't know. It's a kind of no-man's land.

00:27:18.100 --> 00:27:22.733
It seems very strong when you contrast it
with the way we perceive the world -

00:27:22.833 --> 00:27:26.533
- through journalism.

00:27:26.633 --> 00:27:31.833
We think we know what's going on,
right?

00:27:31.933 --> 00:27:34.633
If there's a conflict,
we know what's going on.

00:27:34.733 --> 00:27:38.400
But being there is something else entirely,
and that's what novels can do.

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:42.133
They can open up that space, I think.

00:27:43.233 --> 00:27:47.800
That's a good point.
His name is Serhiy Zhadan.

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:52.033
Now is a good time
to have the first excerpt read out.

00:27:52.133 --> 00:27:56.533
It's from 'Det tredje riket',
which was published in Danish last week.

00:27:56.633 --> 00:28:00.167
It's the third volume
of 'The Morning Star' series.

00:28:00.267 --> 00:28:06.600
The passage we'll hear
tells about a young woman.

00:28:06.700 --> 00:28:08.333
Let's hear it.

00:28:15.233 --> 00:28:17.233
Mom was washing the dishes
when I came down.

00:28:17.333 --> 00:28:20.733
Judging by the sound, Grandma
was in the living room watching TV -

00:28:20.833 --> 00:28:23.100
- if she wasn't just sleeping.

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:26.767
- "I'll take a walk," I said.
- "Do that," Mom said.

00:28:26.867 --> 00:28:28.800
"Where are you going?"

00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:31.667
What did she want with that information?

00:28:31.767 --> 00:28:34.267
"Up to the waterfall, perhaps," I said.

00:28:34.367 --> 00:28:36.800
Mom nodded, and I went outside -

00:28:36.900 --> 00:28:40.867
- toward what Thomas and I had called
the troll forest when we were kids.

00:28:40.967 --> 00:28:44.233
A lot of age-old spruce stood very close -

00:28:44.333 --> 00:28:46.467
- dark even in the middle of the day.

00:28:46.567 --> 00:28:50.900
No, not dark, shadowy. Always quiet.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:53.867
Long veils hanging from the branches.

00:28:53.967 --> 00:28:57.733
Thick moss like blankets on the stones.

00:28:57.833 --> 00:28:59.733
I thought of Valdemar as I walked.

00:28:59.833 --> 00:29:02.000
He should have seen it.

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:04.467
My heart bounced like a tiny hare.

00:29:04.567 --> 00:29:09.133
If he was like everyone else, I'd have taken
a picture of the forest. Sent it to him.

00:29:09.233 --> 00:29:12.200
Just the picture. No comments or anything.

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:14.733
But he's not like everyone else.

00:29:14.833 --> 00:29:15.933
Am I?

00:29:16.867 --> 00:29:19.333
Before I met Valdemar,
I would've said yes.

00:29:19.433 --> 00:29:21.300
Yes, I'm like everyone else.

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:23.633
But he says I'm not like them.

00:29:23.733 --> 00:29:26.800
I'm not so stupid that I don't understand
that's what men tell women -

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:30.233
- when they want them:
"You're not like everyone else."

00:29:30.333 --> 00:29:32.100
"You're very special."

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:36.733
Wind one hour forward and that's it:
"You're lovely," they say.

00:29:36.833 --> 00:29:39.100
But Valdemar is not like that.

00:29:39.200 --> 00:29:41.233
He always says what he means.

00:29:41.333 --> 00:29:45.633
That doesn't mean he's right when
it comes to me. Only that he means it.

00:29:45.733 --> 00:29:49.733
A shadow of despair
followed every thought of Valdemar.

00:29:49.833 --> 00:29:51.900
What should I do?

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:55.267
What on earth should I do?

00:29:55.367 --> 00:29:59.233
Even in there, where the trees
shut out the sun, it was hot.

00:29:59.333 --> 00:30:02.033
Swarms of insects danced in the air.

00:30:02.133 --> 00:30:05.433
In the roar of the waterfall,
dark shadows were moving around.

00:30:05.533 --> 00:30:09.833
Small trout.
I sat down on a rock and looked at them.

00:30:09.933 --> 00:30:13.167
After a few seconds,
I reached into my pocket for the phone -

00:30:13.267 --> 00:30:16.000
- before I remembered
that I'd left it at home.

00:30:16.100 --> 00:30:22.433
Missing it suddenly panged me,
even though I'd left it at home on purpose.

00:30:22.533 --> 00:30:26.267
One of the fish came to the surface,
the back gray and speckled -

00:30:26.367 --> 00:30:28.700
- like the stones around it.

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:31.400
I forced myself to stay seated.

00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:35.133
Looked up the mountainside
where the waterfall came gushing down.

00:30:35.233 --> 00:30:40.600
The trees on top shone in the light
of the last rays of the sun.

00:30:40.700 --> 00:30:44.800
Of all the places in the world,
I always liked best to be here -

00:30:44.900 --> 00:30:46.667
- with Grandma and Grandpa.

00:30:46.767 --> 00:30:49.900
Especially when Thomas and I
were alone with them.

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:51.900
It was pure bliss.

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:54.833
A winter vacation
when the lake was frozen over -

00:30:54.933 --> 00:30:58.833
- and we skated
and got to know the other kids in the area.

00:30:58.933 --> 00:31:02.300
A couple of summer vacations
when Mom and Dad were in Germany.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:06.867
Now Grandpa is dead and Grandma so sick that
she probably won't live through the winter.

00:31:06.967 --> 00:31:11.367
Thomas sat in his room and saw no one.
Dad drank himself to death in Portugal.

00:31:11.467 --> 00:31:15.200
And Mom pretended
that everything was fine.

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:17.733
The hand searched for the phone again.

00:31:17.833 --> 00:31:22.233
A new little slip of disappointment
that it wasn't there.

00:31:22.333 --> 00:31:25.067
I looked over at the woods
on the other side.

00:31:25.167 --> 00:31:27.500
A little further inside,
there were large stones -

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:29.600
- that Grandpa used to say were trolls -

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:33.033
- who hadn't made it into the mountain
before the sun rose.

00:31:33.133 --> 00:31:36.733
We wanted to believe him,
and then it became that way.

00:31:36.833 --> 00:31:42.900
Gleefully, we balanced on the dead
trolls, secure as long as the sun was up.

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:47.667
We asked him if they could come alive
again once the sun went down.

00:31:47.767 --> 00:31:51.700
In a serious voice, he said
he didn't know.

00:31:52.333 --> 00:31:54.533
Dad couldn't stand Mom's father.

00:31:54.633 --> 00:31:58.867
When I was little, I couldn't understand it.
Grandpa was incredible.

00:31:58.967 --> 00:32:04.300
Full of stories that just flowed out of him.
And he laughed so much.

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:09.067
Presumably, he didn't like Dad,
and Dad, of course, understood that.

00:32:09.167 --> 00:32:13.800
And then it became that he didn't like
Grandpa. Not the other way around.

00:32:14.900 --> 00:32:18.167
When Grandma got Parkinson's,
Dad told me it was hereditary -

00:32:18.267 --> 00:32:20.567
- and that if I got it as an adult,
I had to understand -

00:32:20.667 --> 00:32:24.267
- it didn't come from his side
of the family.

00:32:24.367 --> 00:32:27.333
What a thing to tell an eight-year-old.

00:32:27.433 --> 00:32:30.200
The movements
in the waterfall had ceased -

00:32:30.300 --> 00:32:32.833
- and I spent some time
trying to find the fish again.

00:32:32.933 --> 00:32:35.467
They stood perfectly still
just above the bottom.

00:32:35.567 --> 00:32:38.333
I took a rock, bent over the water -

00:32:38.433 --> 00:32:41.600
- and dropped it over one of them
like a depth charge.

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:45.133
It hurried off long before the rock hit it.

00:32:45.233 --> 00:32:47.067
I rinsed my face.

00:32:47.167 --> 00:32:49.600
The water wasn't much cooler
than the warm air -

00:32:49.700 --> 00:32:52.867
- but enough for it to feel cool.

00:32:52.967 --> 00:32:55.933
I didn't feel like going back to Mom
and Grandma -

00:32:56.033 --> 00:32:59.000
- but I couldn't sit there all evening
either.

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:03.267
Had used up my half hour on the phone,
but if I spent half an hour now -

00:33:03.367 --> 00:33:06.833
- I could drop half an hour in the morning.
It would come to the same thing.

00:33:06.933 --> 00:33:10.300
Valdemar had told a story about Hitler.

00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:12.100
They'd talked about charisma.

00:33:12.200 --> 00:33:15.633
Hitler had had a strong charisma,
Gerhardt had said.

00:33:15.733 --> 00:33:18.200
People wanted to be with him.

00:33:18.300 --> 00:33:21.633
Valdemar had read a book
that Hitler's architect had written.

00:33:21.733 --> 00:33:24.867
He was completely infatuated, Valdemar said.

00:33:24.967 --> 00:33:29.967
But when he introduced his father to 'the
man', the father turned completely pale.

00:33:30.067 --> 00:33:34.733
He said Hitler was a dangerous man.
He'd seen something no one else saw.

00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:37.800
"He sounds a bit like Dad," I said.

00:33:37.900 --> 00:33:39.433
Valdemar laughed.

00:33:39.533 --> 00:33:41.467
He didn't do that very often.

00:33:41.567 --> 00:33:44.200
"You live in your own little bubble,"
he said.

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:47.700
I felt stupid. He saw that.

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:51.500
"Hitler was just a human being," he said.
"Just like your father."

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:54.433
Valdemar wasn't a Nazi
though many thought so.

00:33:54.533 --> 00:33:57.600
When he talked about the Third Reich,
he wasn't talking about the Nazis -

00:33:57.700 --> 00:34:00.433
- but something they had believed in
in the Middle Ages.

00:34:00.533 --> 00:34:04.767
That the First Kingdom was the era of God.
The Second Kingdom was the era of Jesus.

00:34:04.867 --> 00:34:07.933
And the Third Kingdom
was the era of the Holy Spirit.

00:34:08.033 --> 00:34:13.933
"We're entering the Third Kingdom,"
he'd said. Not to me, but to the others.

00:34:24.333 --> 00:34:28.900
When you hear this passage,
you get the impression -

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:32.867
- Line, the young woman
has fallen in love with Valdemar -

00:34:32.967 --> 00:34:38.633
- the lead singer in a Norwegian black metal
band who has some very occult ideas...

00:34:38.733 --> 00:34:44.600
You get the impression that this book
deals with some of the same ideas -

00:34:44.700 --> 00:34:47.033
- that you talked about before:

00:34:47.133 --> 00:34:53.367
Being in the moment, being in her thoughts
as it looks in the now.

00:34:53.467 --> 00:34:57.100
I was thinking...

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:05.333
When you wrote this, what...
What is it with this moment?

00:35:05.433 --> 00:35:09.800
In 'The Morning Star', a star has appeared
that wasn't there before.

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:14.733
People think about their private problems,
the ones they've fallen in love with.

00:35:14.833 --> 00:35:19.633
In the second book,
the first part takes place in 1987.

00:35:19.733 --> 00:35:24.100
Chernobyl has just exploded,
and it's mentioned on the radio -

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:27.967
- but it's out in the horizon.
Again, it's the idea of history.

00:35:28.067 --> 00:35:33.167
History, all the important things
that we talk about many years later -

00:35:33.267 --> 00:35:35.233
- take place in the periphery.

00:35:35.333 --> 00:35:40.367
Please talk about
how you use that in this new work.

00:35:40.467 --> 00:35:45.500
- How I use the star?
- History and the moment, I'd say.

00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:49.000
As two opposites.

00:36:04.433 --> 00:36:07.700
I don't know.
Actually, I don't know.

00:36:09.033 --> 00:36:13.833
I'm not thinking in those categories
when I write.

00:36:13.933 --> 00:36:19.900
Here there was a character
that I knew was 19 years old -

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:23.800
- and that I knew had a mother
who took care of her mother.

00:36:25.667 --> 00:36:29.367
And I knew that something had happened.

00:36:29.467 --> 00:36:34.433
And I knew
that it had to be something extreme.

00:36:34.533 --> 00:36:37.867
I didn't know more than that.
I had no background, no nothing.

00:36:37.967 --> 00:36:40.433
She sat down -

00:36:40.533 --> 00:36:45.533
- and then there's the stones
that the grandfather talked about -

00:36:45.633 --> 00:36:48.067
- and then it unfolds.

00:36:48.167 --> 00:36:53.000
Then there's this Valdemar. Who is he?
I don't know.

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:55.233
Then I write when she meets him.

00:36:55.333 --> 00:36:57.800
And there's this Nazi thing about him.

00:36:57.900 --> 00:37:02.267
There were rumors about him. He's one
that people talk about. He's not good.

00:37:02.367 --> 00:37:08.533
But she's in love with him. And that adds
a dynamic that is good to work with, right?

00:37:08.633 --> 00:37:12.667
Very ambivalent, very good feelings,
very terrible feelings.

00:37:12.767 --> 00:37:18.633
He finally invites her to Sweden -

00:37:18.733 --> 00:37:20.267
- to a farm.

00:37:20.367 --> 00:37:23.033
There are lots of people there.
It's almost like a sect.

00:37:23.133 --> 00:37:26.500
It turns out he's playing in a band,
and they're going to perform.

00:37:26.600 --> 00:37:32.067
They've never published their music.
It's a secret.

00:37:32.167 --> 00:37:36.233
But there's this live concert,
and it's absolutely incredible.

00:37:36.333 --> 00:37:42.200
But they're totally anti-commercial.
Totally anti our time.

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:46.600
It comes when I write.

00:37:46.700 --> 00:37:51.133
And then it comes that he's charismatic.

00:37:51.233 --> 00:37:55.833
And then I remember the story
about Hitler and his architect -

00:37:55.933 --> 00:37:58.300
- because it's in his biography.

00:37:58.400 --> 00:38:01.733
He liked Hitler,
and Hitler was charming and charismatic -

00:38:01.833 --> 00:38:04.533
- and then there was his father
who was white as a sheet.

00:38:04.633 --> 00:38:06.733
Something scary.

00:38:06.833 --> 00:38:11.100
I was always attracted to that story,
because it contains this ambivalence.

00:38:11.200 --> 00:38:12.767
And then I use it.

00:38:12.867 --> 00:38:16.900
I take all I've got and throw it all in.

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:20.267
So it sort of appears out of the fog
when you write?

00:38:20.367 --> 00:38:24.267
Yes, all the time.
I never know...

00:38:24.367 --> 00:38:29.400
And many things come from 'My Struggle'.
Many thoughts from 'My Struggle'.

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:31.367
Things I've written in the past.

00:38:31.467 --> 00:38:36.200
But the point is that it all appears
in different characters -

00:38:36.300 --> 00:38:38.667
- so they become different.

00:38:38.767 --> 00:38:41.367
And writing about
a 19-year-old girl in love -

00:38:41.467 --> 00:38:45.300
- I couldn't have done
before I started on this project.

00:38:45.400 --> 00:38:47.667
It would never work.
I wouldn't dare.

00:38:47.767 --> 00:38:50.867
But I thought there was something there,
a tone.

00:38:50.967 --> 00:38:57.833
She's quite innocent, but has her own will.
She wants her own life.

00:38:57.933 --> 00:39:03.233
How that vulnerability
manifests itself in the novel...

00:39:03.333 --> 00:39:09.433
I'm not thinking about the story
or anything else.

00:39:09.533 --> 00:39:13.767
It's incredible to read your works
chronologically -

00:39:13.867 --> 00:39:19.000
- because one begins to see some elements
that appear.

00:39:19.100 --> 00:39:22.233
They're not repetitions, more like...

00:39:22.333 --> 00:39:27.467
They appear like small stones in the sand,
where I think: "I've seen this before."

00:39:27.567 --> 00:39:30.700
It may be a small sentence that is repeated.

00:39:30.800 --> 00:39:34.067
Or a character trait or ideas.

00:39:34.167 --> 00:39:40.767
One idea
I've seen circulating all the way.

00:39:40.867 --> 00:39:45.667
You see it in the angel book. You see
it in 'My Struggle' and in the new one.

00:39:45.767 --> 00:39:50.433
It's the idea that humans create clearings -

00:39:50.533 --> 00:39:54.833
- and then we surrender,
and then darkness arrives.

00:39:54.933 --> 00:39:59.233
You formulate it in different ways,
but it's the idea of the return of evil.

00:39:59.333 --> 00:40:04.500
In the newest work, it's really let loose.

00:40:04.600 --> 00:40:11.533
That idea, why has it been so important
and for so long?

00:40:18.833 --> 00:40:21.033
I don't believe...

00:40:21.133 --> 00:40:25.300
I don't believe in evil as a concept.

00:40:29.633 --> 00:40:31.133
What does that mean?

00:40:31.233 --> 00:40:34.367
I don't believe in a general evil.

00:40:34.467 --> 00:40:39.733
But I believe there is a human trait...

00:40:40.800 --> 00:40:45.633
It's always been an interesting question
for me: "How do we become what we become?"

00:40:45.733 --> 00:40:50.100
In 'The Morning Star', I have a character
called Jostein who's really unpleasant.

00:40:50.200 --> 00:40:57.200
He's sexist, vulgar,
he's everything you don't want to be.

00:40:57.300 --> 00:40:59.467
How did he become like that?

00:40:59.567 --> 00:41:03.033
Once he was two years old and not like that.
What happened?

00:41:03.133 --> 00:41:08.267
There's something in the personality, then
layer upon layer of experiences are added -

00:41:08.367 --> 00:41:12.500
- and you maneuver among them,
and suddenly at 50 you're in a place that...

00:41:12.600 --> 00:41:15.667
You're just there, right?

00:41:15.767 --> 00:41:19.533
But with him I thought okay,
if I reverse it -

00:41:19.633 --> 00:41:23.000
- I'll send him to the land of the dead.

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:27.733
In ancient mythology, there's a thing
called the River of Forgetfulness.

00:41:27.833 --> 00:41:32.300
If you drink from it, you forget everything.
He drinks from it and forgets everything.

00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:34.667
Who is he then?
He doesn't remember anything.

00:41:34.767 --> 00:41:37.633
Where is his character? He has none.

00:41:37.733 --> 00:41:40.533
He's not evil.

00:41:40.633 --> 00:41:44.267
You can imagine that all this comes back.

00:41:44.367 --> 00:41:49.433
It's the characters and what exists
in the characters that interest me.

00:41:50.633 --> 00:41:55.300
But there's another kind of evil,
or you may call it original sin -

00:41:55.400 --> 00:41:58.533
- or something
which is also very interesting:

00:41:58.633 --> 00:42:03.133
We as humans
do a lot of things collectively.

00:42:03.233 --> 00:42:07.600
It doesn't have to be evil,
but the consequences follow like a tail -

00:42:07.700 --> 00:42:10.133
- that we don't know about.

00:42:10.233 --> 00:42:15.500
Like for instance... the car.
We invent the car.

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:19.500
A great invention.
We can go where we want to. Visit people.

00:42:19.600 --> 00:42:22.000
We become very mobile -

00:42:22.100 --> 00:42:26.767
- and acquire a very different culture,
where everything is more accessible.

00:42:26.867 --> 00:42:28.333
It's great.

00:42:28.433 --> 00:42:33.200
And the darkness comes up from behind.
Nobody wanted that. Nobody thought of that.

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:36.033
But now we see it with the climate crisis -

00:42:36.133 --> 00:42:41.233
- which is a kind of connection
between what we do and what happens.

00:42:41.333 --> 00:42:45.633
That which is us and that which will come
have nothing to do with us, individually -

00:42:45.733 --> 00:42:48.000
- but have to do with the collective.

00:42:48.100 --> 00:42:53.867
The French philosopher Michel Serres
talks about that as the original sin.

00:42:53.967 --> 00:42:57.267
That is the original sin.

00:42:57.367 --> 00:43:03.933
You might consider artificial intelligence
that is changing so much.

00:43:04.033 --> 00:43:08.267
It uses the collective.

00:43:08.367 --> 00:43:12.800
There's no individuality.
It uses all the collective that's out there.

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:15.833
It's fed to it,
and then it produces something out of it.

00:43:15.933 --> 00:43:20.433
But it's really operating in something
that's among us.

00:43:20.533 --> 00:43:24.967
We don't know what the consequences are.
We haven't got a clue.

00:43:25.067 --> 00:43:29.100
You say this is a kind of evil.
Impersonal evil.

00:43:29.200 --> 00:43:33.167
The evil consequences
of our collective actions.

00:43:33.267 --> 00:43:37.400
But what mostly interests me
is the one on one.

00:43:37.500 --> 00:43:42.867
What happens to us, and what makes us
what we are and makes us do what we do.

00:43:42.967 --> 00:43:49.167
You can think of heads of state, Putin
or your terrible neighbor -

00:43:49.267 --> 00:43:55.933
- or your good one.
What is actually happening?

00:43:56.033 --> 00:43:59.333
In your new work...

00:43:59.433 --> 00:44:02.433
Your books have always had
a great number of characters -

00:44:02.533 --> 00:44:07.267
- but in this new work one enters the head
of very many people.

00:44:07.367 --> 00:44:12.700
Perhaps it's not a decision -

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:17.033
- but to move from 'My Struggle'
where there's one "I", you might say...

00:44:17.133 --> 00:44:22.167
- to a work where there are,
so far, almost 20.

00:44:22.267 --> 00:44:28.233
Why has this change taken place
in your work?

00:44:28.333 --> 00:44:29.867
That's why.

00:44:29.967 --> 00:44:34.033
Before I'd completely emptied
being one person.

00:44:34.133 --> 00:44:37.900
The whole world is in one person.

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:43.900
There are books, movies, other people,
culture, politics, all kinds of things.

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:50.233
But after all, it's one way
of seeing the world.

00:44:50.333 --> 00:44:54.133
And I thought, everybody's like that.

00:44:54.233 --> 00:44:57.067
If you have different people,
everything's there.

00:44:57.167 --> 00:45:02.067
Then there's another person who's got the
same things but perceives them differently.

00:45:02.167 --> 00:45:05.167
That creates a relationship, a dynamic -

00:45:05.267 --> 00:45:10.700
- where the world starts to look relative.

00:45:11.800 --> 00:45:18.500
If you think about being together
with people that are close to you.

00:45:18.600 --> 00:45:23.133
You know each other really well.
And you start to quarrel.

00:45:23.233 --> 00:45:28.033
Something's happened,
but you see it completely differently.

00:45:28.133 --> 00:45:30.967
It's always fascinated me
how that is possible.

00:45:31.067 --> 00:45:36.967
If we share everything, and then
something's completely opposite.

00:45:37.867 --> 00:45:40.467
The way it's understood.

00:45:43.033 --> 00:45:49.033
In these books with all these characters,
this is exactly what's happening:

00:45:49.133 --> 00:45:52.933
Not who we are for ourselves
but for each other.

00:45:55.233 --> 00:45:59.367
I've been sitting here for perhaps an hour -

00:45:59.467 --> 00:46:01.933
- but I already have a picture
of who your are -

00:46:02.033 --> 00:46:07.000
- and I've heard what you've said.
I created an image of who you are.

00:46:07.100 --> 00:46:10.700
It's not much, but here we are.

00:46:10.800 --> 00:46:17.600
But... I don't have any...

00:46:20.200 --> 00:46:24.300
People show very little
of this enormous inner life -

00:46:24.400 --> 00:46:27.200
- and in a way that's the dynamic
that I write about.

00:46:27.300 --> 00:46:32.700
So for instance, in the book
there's a literature professor, Arne -

00:46:32.800 --> 00:46:36.433
- who's married to an artist called Tove.

00:46:38.800 --> 00:46:44.600
They've been married for a long time.
Arne is a little tired of Tove.

00:46:44.700 --> 00:46:46.333
That's the way it is.

00:46:46.433 --> 00:46:50.200
Then in the third book, you see everything
from Tove's point of view -

00:46:50.300 --> 00:46:52.300
- and everything looks different.

00:46:52.400 --> 00:46:57.933
There's another couple. Kathrine is a
pastor. Gaute is a teacher. They're married.

00:46:58.033 --> 00:47:01.200
You get Kathrine's version, and
in the third book you get Gaute's version -

00:47:01.300 --> 00:47:05.100
- that looks quite different.
But they're looking at the same thing.

00:47:05.200 --> 00:47:11.067
The idea behind 'The Morning Star is
that all these people live their lives -

00:47:11.167 --> 00:47:13.067
- have their opinions -

00:47:13.167 --> 00:47:17.800
- and they look at the same thing outside
that no one knows what is.

00:47:17.900 --> 00:47:24.267
One character is a complete surprise,
of course, the Russian biologist -

00:47:24.367 --> 00:47:27.067
- that suddenly turns up in the second book.

00:47:27.167 --> 00:47:31.500
When I read that, I had to call one of my
friends who was also reading the book -

00:47:31.600 --> 00:47:35.700
- and say: "Listen, it's totally wild.
He's written about a Russian lady."

00:47:35.800 --> 00:47:40.667
In 2023, I don't know why,
but it seemed so wild -

00:47:40.767 --> 00:47:43.833
- particularly
when you come from writing about yourself.

00:47:43.933 --> 00:47:48.000
Was it difficult to write
a Russian woman character?

00:47:48.100 --> 00:47:50.533
Why was she there?

00:47:53.633 --> 00:47:54.767
Yes...

00:47:57.033 --> 00:48:04.067
When I started on the book, 'The Wolves
of Eternity', I didn't know what to write.

00:48:04.167 --> 00:48:06.833
I went through old things I'd written -

00:48:06.933 --> 00:48:13.200
- and found a manuscript from 2012
with 30 pages about a character...

00:48:13.300 --> 00:48:15.933
I'd forgotten that I wrote it,
and I'd never shown it to anyone -

00:48:16.033 --> 00:48:18.467
- because I thought it was so bad.

00:48:18.567 --> 00:48:21.533
Usually, I show everything to my editor,
but not this.

00:48:21.633 --> 00:48:24.967
It's about Syvert.
He came back from the military.

00:48:25.067 --> 00:48:28.800
He had a mother, his father was dead,
and a brother. 30 pages.

00:48:28.900 --> 00:48:34.533
I read it again and thought...
I liked the character.

00:48:34.633 --> 00:48:40.200
He's good-natured, he's jovial,
he's extrovert -

00:48:40.300 --> 00:48:44.300
- all that I'm not,
and that I've written about.

00:48:44.400 --> 00:48:48.233
I thought okay,
I'll see what happens then.

00:48:48.333 --> 00:48:51.800
At the same time,
I read a book about Russia.

00:48:51.900 --> 00:48:57.033
A social anthropologist called Bernstein
had written a book about Russia.

00:48:57.133 --> 00:49:03.200
It mentioned a demonstration in Moscow
against death.

00:49:03.300 --> 00:49:07.567
With posters and speeches.
They were against death.

00:49:09.767 --> 00:49:13.500
I read on and learned that this is actually
a kind of culture -

00:49:13.600 --> 00:49:17.433
- and ideology in Russia -

00:49:17.533 --> 00:49:23.333
- that began at the end of the 1800s with
a specific philosopher called Fyodorov -

00:49:23.433 --> 00:49:26.933
- that Dostoyevsky admired
and that Tolstoy wanted to befriend.

00:49:27.033 --> 00:49:34.900
He was incredibly interesting.
An ascetic who lived for this one idea...

00:49:37.400 --> 00:49:42.033
If all human beings join all their forces
and work on this one thing -

00:49:42.133 --> 00:49:47.533
- perhaps we can raise the dead.
All of them. It should be possible, right?

00:49:47.633 --> 00:49:51.300
We would have to collect all the atoms
and do it.

00:49:51.400 --> 00:49:56.633
It's a crazy idea, but Dostoyevsky
took it seriously. It's about resurrection.

00:49:56.733 --> 00:50:01.100
Since then, this idea has existed
in Russian culture.

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:06.467
As far as the novel is concerned,
I have a Norwegian person, 20 years old -

00:50:06.567 --> 00:50:09.800
- a nice guy -

00:50:09.900 --> 00:50:13.467
- and the novel has to go to Russia,
because I want to write about this.

00:50:13.567 --> 00:50:15.733
So I had to find the way to go there -

00:50:15.833 --> 00:50:19.800
- and then the father, who's dead,
has exchanged letters -

00:50:19.900 --> 00:50:23.300
- with a Russian woman.

00:50:23.400 --> 00:50:25.967
I go to Russia -

00:50:26.067 --> 00:50:30.400
- and there was a room,
and I knew she'd be around 40.

00:50:30.500 --> 00:50:36.433
I'm looking down. I didn't even know
what I had to work with.

00:50:36.533 --> 00:50:41.833
I'm looking out at the street,
it's snowing in Moscow.

00:50:41.933 --> 00:50:45.633
She hears someone in the next room.
It's the son, he's 19.

00:50:45.733 --> 00:50:49.733
I'm thinking, biologist, perhaps, because
I'm interested in the beginning of life -

00:50:49.833 --> 00:50:52.833
- instead of death like in the first book.

00:50:52.933 --> 00:50:56.133
Where are you going?
You'll be lecturing at the university.

00:50:56.233 --> 00:50:58.933
Basic studies,
because she's hasn't been doing so well.

00:50:59.033 --> 00:51:01.033
Then it's on its way.

00:51:01.133 --> 00:51:07.333
I was in Russia for nine days,
and I used the nine days to the full -

00:51:07.433 --> 00:51:12.600
- so everything is authentic with locations,
etc.

00:51:12.700 --> 00:51:18.933
It was very risky.
I have no idea about how a woman thinks.

00:51:19.033 --> 00:51:21.633
I have no idea how a Russian thinks.
or about biology.

00:51:21.733 --> 00:51:25.900
But I threw myself at it -

00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:29.400
- and then I sent
the manuscript to my cousin -

00:51:29.500 --> 00:51:33.633
- who's lived in Russia and speaks Russian
and to my Russian translator -

00:51:33.733 --> 00:51:37.000
- and asked them to read it and comment it.

00:51:37.100 --> 00:51:39.633
The comments were extremely practical.

00:51:39.733 --> 00:51:43.100
The practical is always
where you make mistakes.

00:51:43.200 --> 00:51:47.667
The character takes the train in Russia,
goes to the restroom -

00:51:47.767 --> 00:51:51.100
- and there's toilet paper.
It would never happen.

00:51:51.200 --> 00:51:54.667
So the toilet paper is written out.

00:51:54.767 --> 00:51:56.300
Things like that.

00:51:56.400 --> 00:52:03.900
Now we'll return to the angel book,
'A Time for Everything'.

00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:10.133
You write something quite amazing about
this fictional angel researcher, Bellori.

00:52:10.233 --> 00:52:15.200
You write that to understand him
one has to know his time.

00:52:15.300 --> 00:52:21.733
An author can learn something about
someone by understanding who they were -

00:52:21.833 --> 00:52:26.333
- but the most interesting is their time
and the place they hail from.

00:52:26.433 --> 00:52:29.800
You also wrote the same
in an essay about Dag Solstad.

00:52:29.900 --> 00:52:34.067
If you were to explain, what is your time?

00:52:34.167 --> 00:52:38.033
I don't mean the entire epoch -

00:52:38.133 --> 00:52:44.467
- but do you have a special time
that is more yours than other times?

00:52:51.067 --> 00:52:58.900
I do. Because when I started on the second
book, 'The Wolves of Eternity', it's 1986 -

00:52:59.000 --> 00:53:03.300
- I was about 18-19 years old...

00:53:03.400 --> 00:53:09.633
Wait, 18-19... 1986... Yes, 19.

00:53:09.733 --> 00:53:11.800
18-19.

00:53:11.900 --> 00:53:17.333
And the book I'm writing now, that's lucky,
because it also takes place in 1986.

00:53:17.433 --> 00:53:20.100
In London.

00:53:20.200 --> 00:53:28.267
But it's because I know that time
a hundred percent, somehow.

00:53:28.367 --> 00:53:31.833
It's quite natural.

00:53:31.933 --> 00:53:37.867
I think it's because when you're 20,
your world is finalized.

00:53:37.967 --> 00:53:41.500
You're in the world in a way.
You're in control.

00:53:41.600 --> 00:53:44.567
And then you're mistaken,
because the world changes -

00:53:44.667 --> 00:53:48.267
- but you only actually have what you had
when you were 20.

00:53:48.367 --> 00:53:53.833
Everything else becomes a little panicky.
You constantly have to catch up.

00:53:53.933 --> 00:54:00.833
I think it's when you're 20
that your time is created.

00:54:00.933 --> 00:54:05.400
Everything else is
things that come against you.

00:54:05.500 --> 00:54:08.867
- Aftertime.
- Yes, I don't quite know what that is.

00:54:08.967 --> 00:54:14.533
It's just my theory. That's how I see it.
And mentally, I stopped when I was 20.

00:54:14.633 --> 00:54:19.500
What about place? You've lived outside
of Norway a great part of your life.

00:54:19.600 --> 00:54:23.567
You called it an exile. You lived in Sweden,
now you live in London.

00:54:23.667 --> 00:54:27.633
What is the real location for you?

00:54:29.967 --> 00:54:32.367
There are two locations, I think.

00:54:32.467 --> 00:54:40.167
One is the place I hail from,
Southern Norway.

00:54:40.267 --> 00:54:44.667
The other place is home, the family.

00:54:44.767 --> 00:54:48.200
That's what is home in a way.

00:54:48.300 --> 00:54:51.700
Some have defined home as a place -

00:54:51.800 --> 00:54:56.233
- where they can't deny you entry.

00:54:57.333 --> 00:54:59.333
That's home.

00:54:59.433 --> 00:55:05.333
It is the family, and it may be anywhere.
It may be London or somewhere else.

00:55:05.433 --> 00:55:11.533
You're one of the authors that I know
who've used most space -

00:55:11.633 --> 00:55:13.767
- to describe the art of others -

00:55:13.867 --> 00:55:17.667
- and describe how it affects you.

00:55:17.767 --> 00:55:23.733
 It's this interest
in what meeting great art does to you.

00:55:23.833 --> 00:55:27.533
Can you talk about what it does -

00:55:27.633 --> 00:55:32.367
- when you see a picture or read a book -

00:55:32.467 --> 00:55:35.700
- or listen to music?

00:55:35.800 --> 00:55:39.267
Yes, it...

00:55:41.767 --> 00:55:46.567
I've always thought it's so strange -

00:55:46.667 --> 00:55:52.300
- that a picture
can arouse such strong feelings.

00:55:52.400 --> 00:55:57.100
You've probably tried to draw
and paint yourself. Me too.

00:55:57.200 --> 00:56:00.233
Nothing happens.

00:56:00.333 --> 00:56:03.833
But then you may look at a picture
of an apple, for instance -

00:56:03.933 --> 00:56:10.400
- or a glass of water...
actually, anything -

00:56:10.500 --> 00:56:16.700
- and it arouses so much,
and therefore I try to write about it -

00:56:16.800 --> 00:56:22.667
- try to find out what's at stake, actually.

00:56:22.767 --> 00:56:25.733
When you write novels you use words,
concepts -

00:56:25.833 --> 00:56:28.867
- you use intellect, thoughts.

00:56:28.967 --> 00:56:31.967
But you don't communicate directly.

00:56:32.067 --> 00:56:36.867
Music and visual arts
go directly to the heart.

00:56:36.967 --> 00:56:40.367
It's actually only feelings, nothing else.

00:56:40.467 --> 00:56:46.333
That's a fundamental question:
What is it? Why?

00:56:46.433 --> 00:56:48.500
That's the starting point -

00:56:48.600 --> 00:56:54.100
- and then I wrote about specific arts
that are very different.

00:56:55.767 --> 00:57:01.267
Do you think one becomes a better
human being by looking at great art?

00:57:01.367 --> 00:57:03.500
Or read great art?

00:57:03.600 --> 00:57:07.233
If you do that... I don't think
you become a better human being -

00:57:07.333 --> 00:57:10.967
- but I think
the world becomes less simple -

00:57:11.067 --> 00:57:12.533
- and that's important -

00:57:12.633 --> 00:57:16.900
- because the simple answers,
the simple solutions -

00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:19.300
- simple politics -

00:57:19.400 --> 00:57:22.833
- that's no good in art.

00:57:22.933 --> 00:57:29.167
Everything becomes complicated
and multi-faceted -

00:57:29.267 --> 00:57:33.433
- and that's a good thing.
It's one of the things that art really does.

00:57:33.533 --> 00:57:36.700
It's a little like
what I said about the novel:

00:57:36.800 --> 00:57:42.233
It enters before the world is created,
when it's still being created.

00:57:45.267 --> 00:57:47.900
I've said that:

00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:54.367
Real art is the opposite of Nazism.
Absolutely the opposite.

00:57:54.467 --> 00:58:00.833
Though Nazism too operates
with very strong feelings, obviously.

00:58:00.933 --> 00:58:05.467
We'll now again listen to Benjamin.

00:58:05.567 --> 00:58:08.533
He'll read
from the sixth volume of 'My Struggle'.

00:58:08.633 --> 00:58:11.067
In this passage -

00:58:11.167 --> 00:58:17.400
- Karl Ove, the story-teller,
is talking to his friend Geir.

00:58:17.500 --> 00:58:20.533
The children are running around.
They're preparing food.

00:58:20.633 --> 00:58:24.933
And they're talking about various things.
That's what we'll hear now.

00:58:31.100 --> 00:58:35.467
"Do you want a beer or what?" Geir said
when I got back into the kitchen.

00:58:35.567 --> 00:58:37.933
"Why not?"

00:58:38.033 --> 00:58:42.633
"So we're structuring the social space,"
he said, opening the refrigerator door.

00:58:42.733 --> 00:58:45.067
I sat down. He passed me a beer.

00:58:45.167 --> 00:58:47.867
"We thoroughly design the physical space."

00:58:47.967 --> 00:58:53.800
"We have elevated awareness of everything.
Who designed the fork we eat with, we know."

00:58:53.900 --> 00:58:58.567
"We eliminate all dangers
and take precautions for everything."

00:58:58.667 --> 00:59:01.700
"What disappears is spontaneity."

00:59:01.800 --> 00:59:06.300
"Why do we want spontaneity to go away?
What do we achieve by that?"

00:59:06.400 --> 00:59:12.233
"Spontaneity is unpredictable. It can't be
repeated. Repetition is the key to control."

00:59:12.333 --> 00:59:14.433
"There we are."

00:59:14.533 --> 00:59:17.300
"In a way, the circle is joined," I said.

00:59:17.400 --> 00:59:18.767
"What do you mean?"

00:59:18.867 --> 00:59:21.267
"Neanderthals," I said.

00:59:21.367 --> 00:59:24.100
"Your favorite subject."

00:59:24.200 --> 00:59:29.167
"What insight have you gleaned
from their sorry lives today, I wonder?"

00:59:29.267 --> 00:59:31.467
"Do you think they were spontaneous?"

00:59:31.567 --> 00:59:34.367
"When you ask like that,
the answer is probably no."

00:59:34.467 --> 00:59:40.367
"During the 200,000 years they lived
in Europe, they didn't develop at all."

00:59:40.467 --> 00:59:43.700
"They did exactly the same thing
when they came, in exactly the same way -

00:59:43.800 --> 00:59:48.100
- as they did when they disappeared.
They even lived in exactly the same places."

00:59:48.200 --> 00:59:52.867
"In a cave in France,
a tribe lived for 40,000 years."

00:59:52.967 --> 00:59:55.767
"They only disappeared
when the cave collapsed."

00:59:55.867 --> 00:59:59.867
"It's like one family living on a farm
for forty thousand years."

00:59:59.967 --> 01:00:03.200
"It is utterly unthinkable.
But not for them."

01:00:03.300 --> 01:00:07.667
"They made the same tools. They hunted
the same way. They ate the same food."

01:00:07.767 --> 01:00:10.867
"Not one thing about them changed."

01:00:10.967 --> 01:00:16.500
"It's fascinating, isn't it? That our
closest relatives were unable to change."

01:00:16.600 --> 01:00:21.267
"That progress was a totally
foreign concept to them."

01:00:21.367 --> 01:00:25.700
"No improvisation. No spontaneity.
It didn't exist."

01:00:25.800 --> 01:00:31.133
"The first humans represented
an unimaginable revolution when they came."

01:00:31.233 --> 01:00:36.233
"What separated us from them was precisely
what we are now trying to remove."

01:00:36.333 --> 01:00:39.433
"Now you have to say it's not so long
since the first humans arrived."

01:00:39.533 --> 01:00:41.667
"Yes, but it isn't."

01:00:41.767 --> 01:00:47.133
"Back then many different kinds of humans
lived side by side. It'll happen again."

01:00:47.233 --> 01:00:50.667
"If you're thinking, let's say,
three hundred thousand years ahead."

01:00:50.767 --> 01:00:55.533
"There should be other types of humans.
Perhaps already after sixty thousand years."

01:00:55.633 --> 01:00:58.100
"Yes, give them my regards."

01:00:58.200 --> 01:01:02.300
"Ha ha. But do you know how many
Neanderthals there were?"

01:01:02.400 --> 01:01:03.933
"No."

01:01:04.033 --> 01:01:07.700
"A high guess will be 20,000.
A low one, ten thousand."

01:01:07.800 --> 01:01:11.467
"Somewhere in between.
And that was all of them."

01:01:11.567 --> 01:01:14.467
"Just think what Europe looked like."

01:01:14.567 --> 01:01:19.367
"Totally without humans.
Only animals and birds, forests and plains."

01:01:19.467 --> 01:01:26.033
"And then 15,000 stubborn Neanderthals
scattered in caves around the continent."

01:01:26.133 --> 01:01:29.500
- "Your utopia?"
- "Almost."

01:01:29.600 --> 01:01:32.867
"But the main difference between
the first humans and the Neanderthals..."

01:01:32.967 --> 01:01:35.167
"You know what it was?"

01:01:35.267 --> 01:01:38.500
"They didn't have frankfurters."

01:01:38.600 --> 01:01:41.067
"Humans wore jewelry."

01:01:41.167 --> 01:01:45.533
"They wore teeth from the dead around
their necks. They thought in symbols."

01:01:45.633 --> 01:01:47.633
"There are more things in Heaven and Earth."

01:01:47.733 --> 01:01:50.867
"It was an unthinkable thought
for the Neanderthals."

01:01:50.967 --> 01:01:57.167
"One wonders what the idea of it all is,"
Geir said. "Teeth around the neck."

01:01:57.267 --> 01:02:02.567
- "It means that something more exists."
- "That's precisely what makes me think."

01:02:02.667 --> 01:02:04.467
"The teeth are just teeth."

01:02:04.567 --> 01:02:08.867
"The Neanderthal existence in the world
seems to me more adequate."

01:02:08.967 --> 01:02:12.233
"There are probably Neanderthal genes
in our DNA."

01:02:12.333 --> 01:02:17.133
"Not in Africans', since there were
no humans when Neanderthals left Africa."

01:02:17.233 --> 01:02:20.600
"But in Europeans' DNA.
I don't think Neanderthals went extinct."

01:02:20.700 --> 01:02:24.467
"I think they mingled with humans
and disappeared into them."

01:02:24.567 --> 01:02:26.267
"You think so?"

01:02:26.367 --> 01:02:29.267
"Yes. At least that's not implausible."

01:02:29.367 --> 01:02:32.767
"And so far, Neanderthals have been here
longer than us."

01:02:32.867 --> 01:02:37.167
- "But they didn't know about that."
- "No, they probably didn't."

01:02:37.267 --> 01:02:42.033
"So we've gone from a world without
mystery to a world full of mysteries -

01:02:42.133 --> 01:02:46.100
- and back to a world
without mystery again."

01:02:46.200 --> 01:02:49.733
"It's incredible that the state pays
people like you to sit and ponder all day -

01:02:49.833 --> 01:02:52.867
- and then write books
about what you've thought about."

01:02:52.967 --> 01:02:57.367
"There was a small human race on Flores,
an island in Indonesia."

01:02:57.467 --> 01:03:03.033
"They lived in a cave. Probably, they were
no more than 50 to 100 individuals."

01:03:03.133 --> 01:03:05.167
"About a meter tall."

01:03:05.267 --> 01:03:07.567
- "Dwarfs?"
- "No."

01:03:07.667 --> 01:03:10.667
"They were simply a completely different
human race."

01:03:10.767 --> 01:03:13.133
"They lived there
well into the age of humans."

01:03:13.233 --> 01:03:16.300
"And among those who live there now,
strange stories are circulating -

01:03:16.400 --> 01:03:19.633
- about little people
stealing their vegetables?"

01:03:19.733 --> 01:03:21.367
"I heard a tale like that."

01:03:21.467 --> 01:03:25.633
"He said the women threw their breasts
over their shoulders when they ran."

01:03:25.733 --> 01:03:28.033
"Where the hell did that detail come from?"

01:03:28.133 --> 01:03:32.133
"When he told about them, they
sounded like some kind of little trolls -

01:03:32.233 --> 01:03:37.233
- or, yes, creatures from mythology.
Infernal or something."

01:03:37.333 --> 01:03:42.833
"But then they found little skeletons
in the cave. Small mini humans."

01:03:42.933 --> 01:03:45.233
"Where do you know all this from?"

01:03:45.333 --> 01:03:50.100
"They broadcast such documentaries at night.
I look a lot at things like that."

01:03:50.200 --> 01:03:54.433
"I don't know how much is true and how much
is nonsense, but it's incredibly exciting."

01:03:54.533 --> 01:03:56.533
"Yes, apparently."

01:03:56.633 --> 01:04:01.700
"It's about the utopians, I think.
It was actually different then."

01:04:01.800 --> 01:04:04.567
"Not only the environment,
but also the human itself."

01:04:04.667 --> 01:04:07.933
"I just want alternatives.
Anything actually."

01:04:08.033 --> 01:04:11.567
"So you want to be a Neanderthal.
Is that what you're saying?"

01:04:11.667 --> 01:04:17.333
"No. But the fact that history is over
and there is no other future -

01:04:17.433 --> 01:04:20.100
- except the repetition
of what we have now -

01:04:20.200 --> 01:04:23.233
- every now then makes me almost sick
with claustrophobia."

01:04:23.333 --> 01:04:27.700
"I don't necessarily have to do anything
else or be anything else. That's not it."

01:04:27.800 --> 01:04:32.200
"But I want the possibility
that a completely different life exists."

01:04:32.300 --> 01:04:37.033
"We live in a period of zero visions.
It also means we have zero visions."

01:04:37.133 --> 01:04:40.033
"You got lucky with that one."

01:04:40.133 --> 01:04:43.933
I got up and went into the living room.
Geir followed.

01:04:44.033 --> 01:04:48.300
'Bolibompa' had finished.
Now they were looking a youth program.

01:04:48.400 --> 01:04:52.733
I switched the TV off and looked at Geir.
"Should we give them a bath?"

01:05:03.333 --> 01:05:08.567
This is such a cool passage.
Absolutely wild.

01:05:08.667 --> 01:05:12.567
There are several reasons
why I included it today.

01:05:12.667 --> 01:05:18.000
One is that it contains the wording:
"I just want alternatives."

01:05:18.100 --> 01:05:22.133
It's one of the ideas
that are repeated in your work:

01:05:22.233 --> 01:05:28.267
The yearning for something different.
The idea that another world is possible.

01:05:28.367 --> 01:05:35.233
Why do you keep saying that
and make your characters shout it out?

01:05:35.333 --> 01:05:38.533
Because I feel it so strongly.

01:05:48.033 --> 01:05:51.733
You never know, if you feel something -

01:05:51.833 --> 01:05:57.633
- if that feeling is general
or if it's just personal.

01:05:57.733 --> 01:06:00.767
But I've had the feeling -

01:06:00.867 --> 01:06:05.733
- that the future
will just be more of the same.

01:06:05.833 --> 01:06:10.033
We can't imagine another future -

01:06:10.133 --> 01:06:13.667
- or that something will happen
that will change -

01:06:13.767 --> 01:06:17.400
- or that there'll be
a kind of utopian dimension.

01:06:17.500 --> 01:06:21.400
I've got the feeling that this is it -

01:06:21.500 --> 01:06:24.667
- and there'll be more of this.
Things won't change.

01:06:24.767 --> 01:06:28.267
But the crazy thing is that
things have never changed as much -

01:06:28.367 --> 01:06:32.467
- as they have over the last 10-15 years.
It's boom, boom, new things constantly.

01:06:32.567 --> 01:06:35.233
Our entire social world
has been turned upside down -

01:06:35.333 --> 01:06:37.733
- and yet I feel and think:

01:06:37.833 --> 01:06:40.867
It'll never change.
It'll always be the same.

01:06:40.967 --> 01:06:45.800
And that is something
I want to write about and that interests me.

01:06:45.900 --> 01:06:51.100
With those feelings, I began to read -

01:06:51.200 --> 01:06:58.467
- an English music journalist
and academic and theorist -

01:06:58.567 --> 01:07:01.833
- Mark Fisher.

01:07:01.933 --> 01:07:07.400
He writes a lot about
what he calls the hauntology -

01:07:07.500 --> 01:07:12.500
- that the past comes up from the back
and takes us.

01:07:12.600 --> 01:07:17.367
With technology, it's as if the past
is in the present -

01:07:17.467 --> 01:07:19.067
- accessible.

01:07:19.167 --> 01:07:24.667
Popular culture is the 90s or the 2000s,
we can use everything -

01:07:24.767 --> 01:07:27.367
- concurrently, in parallel, and we do.

01:07:27.467 --> 01:07:30.533
In a way, it's as if the past
has taken over the entire space.

01:07:30.633 --> 01:07:32.867
There's no room for the future.

01:07:32.967 --> 01:07:41.000
It's a sentiment. It's not real.
It's just a feeling that I write about.

01:07:41.100 --> 01:07:46.467
But what was so upsetting by rereading
this passage from 'My Struggle' -

01:07:46.567 --> 01:07:51.933
- was reading about the idea
that we're at the end of history and say:

01:07:52.033 --> 01:07:54.300
"Yes, that's how it once was."

01:07:54.400 --> 01:07:56.767
Then you bring out
the 'Morning Star' series -

01:07:56.867 --> 01:08:01.433
- and formulate
an entirely new perception of time -

01:08:01.533 --> 01:08:08.000
- similar to what Solvej Balle does:
something's happening these years.

01:08:08.100 --> 01:08:12.933
The feeling that the future is out there,
and we don't know what's coming.

01:08:13.033 --> 01:08:18.800
It's strange to read a book
where suddenly a morning star appears.

01:08:18.900 --> 01:08:22.100
It's supernatural.

01:08:22.200 --> 01:08:25.600
But it's as if you'd written
a realistic novel -

01:08:25.700 --> 01:08:30.300
- it would've felt more fictional
than the supernatural.

01:08:30.400 --> 01:08:36.333
The supernatural novel expresses
what it feels like to be human right now.

01:08:40.400 --> 01:08:44.567
It's funny that you mention Solvej Balle.

01:08:45.667 --> 01:08:49.300
Two years ago I was in Copenhagen.

01:08:49.400 --> 01:08:53.700
I'd heard about Solvej Balle in the 90s
and had read about her -

01:08:53.800 --> 01:08:56.067
- and I saw a book in a store and bought it.

01:08:56.167 --> 01:09:00.067
I started to read,
and read three books in one go.

01:09:00.167 --> 01:09:03.733
Fabulous. Absolutely
incredibly, incredibly, incredibly good.

01:09:03.833 --> 01:09:10.567
When I read it I didn't know why.
"I just have to read this."

01:09:10.667 --> 01:09:14.567
From any point of view,
it's an absolutely incredibly good series.

01:09:14.667 --> 01:09:18.333
But what happens?
The same day returns again and again.

01:09:18.433 --> 01:09:20.433
There's is no future, right?

01:09:20.533 --> 01:09:24.300
It's exactly the same feeling,
just expressed so much better -

01:09:24.400 --> 01:09:28.300
- and precisely,
as a metaphor and a perfect novel -

01:09:28.400 --> 01:09:31.233
- because that's what a perfect novel does.

01:09:31.333 --> 01:09:36.133
It manages to encompass the entire epoch,
the entire emotion in one image.

01:09:36.233 --> 01:09:38.767
And then you continue with that.

01:09:38.867 --> 01:09:41.600
It took almost a year
after I'd read that book...

01:09:41.700 --> 01:09:47.533
That's it. That's what it's about. That's
why I like it so much. Among other things.

01:09:47.633 --> 01:09:51.000
If she'd done like me and said:

01:09:51.100 --> 01:09:54.467
"Blah-blah-blah, the future blah-blah,"
it wouldn't work.

01:09:54.567 --> 01:09:58.333
But here you get a great,
existential experience -

01:09:58.433 --> 01:10:03.100
- from reading this fictional work.

01:10:03.200 --> 01:10:09.933
It has such an appeal.
Don't you agree?

01:10:11.033 --> 01:10:13.933
Perhaps yours too. I think so.

01:10:14.033 --> 01:10:19.033
Do you see why the two works...

01:10:19.133 --> 01:10:23.867
Do you see the connection to your own
work when you think of Solvej Balle?

01:10:23.967 --> 01:10:27.633
Or do you see her
as something completely different?

01:10:27.733 --> 01:10:29.667
If I see the connection
between Solvej Balle...?

01:10:29.767 --> 01:10:34.267
Between 'The Morning Star' and her book.

01:10:37.867 --> 01:10:41.000
On the on hand, when I read her book -

01:10:41.100 --> 01:10:45.533
- I just read
and think it's absolutely fabulous.

01:10:45.633 --> 01:10:50.633
On the other, I'm incredibly jealous.
"Why don't I do that?"

01:10:50.733 --> 01:10:53.833
I throw away all my energy on this,
while this exists.

01:10:53.933 --> 01:10:57.100
Really, that's how it feels.

01:10:57.200 --> 01:11:01.033
It's terrible.

01:11:01.133 --> 01:11:04.667
I still think it's good that you read it.

01:11:04.767 --> 01:11:08.367
We were to talk in November.
Then it was canceled.

01:11:08.467 --> 01:11:12.300
In a way I'm very happy that happened
and that we're sitting here now -

01:11:12.400 --> 01:11:16.567
- because in the meantime something
absolutely wild has happened -

01:11:16.667 --> 01:11:19.033
- which is artificial intelligence -

01:11:19.133 --> 01:11:24.267
- and the development that started
in December when ChatGPT came out.

01:11:24.367 --> 01:11:27.633
Almost every day something new is happening.

01:11:27.733 --> 01:11:33.400
I think that development reveals something
else in your work -

01:11:33.500 --> 01:11:37.833
- which is that from the beginning
it was about human consciousness.

01:11:37.933 --> 01:11:40.833
It was about the human essence,
you might say.

01:11:40.933 --> 01:11:43.300
It's been about what thoughts look like.

01:11:43.400 --> 01:11:46.300
That's a thing you explore
in 'Det tredje riket'.

01:11:46.400 --> 01:11:51.300
When you think about
the consequences of all these new things -

01:11:51.400 --> 01:11:55.567
- can you say something about that?

01:11:55.667 --> 01:12:00.500
What these books are about,
somewhat in the background -

01:12:00.600 --> 01:12:03.200
- but that I want to incorporate -

01:12:03.300 --> 01:12:08.667
- is quite simply
that man and machine grow together.

01:12:08.767 --> 01:12:10.233
They get ever closer.

01:12:10.333 --> 01:12:15.933
Technology and biology come nearer
and nearer and infiltrate each other.

01:12:16.033 --> 01:12:19.567
It's terrible to work on such a project,
and then something like this happens -

01:12:19.667 --> 01:12:25.167
- which is like a new step of the machine
closing in on consciousness.

01:12:25.267 --> 01:12:29.633
This is the dystopian element
in these books, I thought -

01:12:29.733 --> 01:12:34.500
- but reality is more dystopian.

01:12:36.633 --> 01:12:40.367
What should I say...?

01:12:40.467 --> 01:12:45.967
Six months ago, a friend of mine...
He works with technology.

01:12:46.067 --> 01:12:51.467
He said, "Soon machines
will be able to write your books."

01:12:51.567 --> 01:12:56.100
I said, "No, come on. Don't be stupid.
That's ridiculous."

01:12:56.200 --> 01:12:59.333
"Are you such a technological optimist?"

01:12:59.433 --> 01:13:02.800
Then this happens, and he sends me a text -

01:13:02.900 --> 01:13:08.633
- where he's asked artificial intelligence
to write in my style.

01:13:08.733 --> 01:13:12.733
And I receive a text in my style
written by a machine.

01:13:12.833 --> 01:13:15.367
- Was it good?
- What should I say?

01:13:18.367 --> 01:13:21.467
It lacks soul.

01:13:23.500 --> 01:13:26.233
But it's...

01:13:27.333 --> 01:13:34.233
But one issue when talking about novels is,
will people stop writing novels?

01:13:34.333 --> 01:13:38.567
No, the opposite will happen.
Precisely the opposite will happen.

01:13:38.667 --> 01:13:44.833
The human, the attentive, which before was
called the soul will be much more important.

01:13:44.933 --> 01:13:51.500
Obviously, what artificial intelligence does
is to collect what already exists.

01:13:51.600 --> 01:13:58.467
It's like an enormous sea of the collective
subconscious that it feeds on.

01:13:58.567 --> 01:14:05.267
But it can't be attentive
like a human being.

01:14:05.367 --> 01:14:11.233
If it gets to that...
many believe that it will become conscious -

01:14:11.333 --> 01:14:13.833
- then it'll be a human.

01:14:13.933 --> 01:14:19.167
But what's interesting,
and what these books deal with is -

01:14:19.267 --> 01:14:22.433
- if we can imagine
other kinds of intelligence.

01:14:22.533 --> 01:14:27.067
Could we think that the forest
is one type of intelligence -

01:14:27.167 --> 01:14:30.467
- which is so outlandish that
we don't even understand it as intelligence?

01:14:30.567 --> 01:14:34.533
It's not long ago that we started
to understand that trees have a memory.

01:14:34.633 --> 01:14:39.933
Trees may actually have something
similar to thoughts.

01:14:40.033 --> 01:14:43.933
It was unthinkable 15 years ago.
Now it's accepted.

01:14:44.033 --> 01:14:48.500
We know much more about how it happens.
We know that trees communicate.

01:14:48.600 --> 01:14:50.233
And...

01:14:52.233 --> 01:14:58.700
Is artificial intelligence another form of
intelligence, and we don't know what it is?

01:14:58.800 --> 01:15:03.433
The problem is that we actually don't know
what ordinary intelligence is.

01:15:03.533 --> 01:15:05.633
We don't know what consciousness is.

01:15:05.733 --> 01:15:09.800
I've studied consciousness, because a person
is studying consciousness in this book -

01:15:09.900 --> 01:15:13.800
- and so I've taken an interest.

01:15:13.900 --> 01:15:17.267
The more you read, the more you realize
that no one knows what it is.

01:15:17.367 --> 01:15:22.567
No one knows what it takes. At the same
time, it's the only thing we have.

01:15:22.667 --> 01:15:26.067
Our only value is consciousness,
because that's what we see the world with.

01:15:26.167 --> 01:15:29.467
It's a total mystery.

01:15:30.600 --> 01:15:32.633
Just a simple thing like that.

01:15:36.367 --> 01:15:41.000
The novel has always been
this strange parasite -

01:15:41.100 --> 01:15:45.200
- that jumped onto people
throughout history -

01:15:45.300 --> 01:15:49.167
- and then the form changes
along the way.

01:15:49.267 --> 01:15:54.267
If we say that this development
with artificial intelligence...

01:15:54.367 --> 01:15:57.567
People, authors keep writing novels.

01:15:57.667 --> 01:16:03.100
Could you imagine that an inner change
will take place in the novel?

01:16:03.200 --> 01:16:09.567
What I mean is that over the course
of the 20th century, God died -

01:16:09.667 --> 01:16:15.300
- and most authors related to the fact
that there was not this outer force.

01:16:15.400 --> 01:16:17.967
That also influenced
how novels were written -

01:16:18.067 --> 01:16:22.200
- because the external voice
did not make sense anymore.

01:16:22.300 --> 01:16:29.967
If we accept that artificial intelligence
exists now... It's an external voice.

01:16:30.067 --> 01:16:35.667
To explain something,
you need something on a higher level.

01:16:35.767 --> 01:16:39.567
Do you think it'll change the novel
in this way?

01:16:42.333 --> 01:16:45.567
If you think again about 'Madame Bovary' -

01:16:45.667 --> 01:16:50.767
- the question is what it's about.
It's difficult to say.

01:16:50.867 --> 01:16:57.800
In essence, it's about reality -

01:16:57.900 --> 01:16:59.833
- and a depiction of reality.

01:16:59.933 --> 01:17:04.267
Reality and a fiction
meeting in the fiction.

01:17:05.333 --> 01:17:09.967
You might ask, "What is real?"

01:17:10.067 --> 01:17:12.800
And 'Don Quixote', the first novel,
what is that about?

01:17:12.900 --> 01:17:18.933
It's about someone
who has a fantasy image of reality -

01:17:19.033 --> 01:17:20.867
- that crashes against reality.

01:17:20.967 --> 01:17:22.700
That's the first novel.

01:17:22.800 --> 01:17:24.533
What is real?

01:17:24.633 --> 01:17:28.667
That's the novel's question -

01:17:28.767 --> 01:17:33.067
- which it can elaborate on in all kinds
of ways and forms and genres, etc.

01:17:33.167 --> 01:17:38.733
But it will be even more essential
to ask oneself about that now:

01:17:38.833 --> 01:17:42.433
What is real?
What does it mean to be real?

01:17:42.533 --> 01:17:46.267
What difference will it make to read
a book written by artificial intelligence -

01:17:46.367 --> 01:17:49.633
- if it moves me?
If it makes me laugh?

01:17:49.733 --> 01:17:53.333
Where are we?
What is real?

01:17:53.433 --> 01:17:57.933
That's the novel's real question.

01:17:58.033 --> 01:18:02.967
I don't think it'll change anything,
it'll just make the conflict more critical.

01:18:03.067 --> 01:18:06.433
Is there something you haven't yet
written about where you think:

01:18:06.533 --> 01:18:09.500
"I have to write about this?"

01:18:19.333 --> 01:18:20.467
Yes...

01:18:26.467 --> 01:18:34.267
Yes, there is.
But it's more that I want to be wilder.

01:18:34.367 --> 01:18:37.667
For instance, I love 'Dracula'.
Bram Stoker's 'Dracula'.

01:18:37.767 --> 01:18:43.033
I'd love to be completely gothic and wild -

01:18:43.133 --> 01:18:46.933
- while it has to be believable.
It has to work.

01:18:48.933 --> 01:18:52.833
When I started on 'Morning Star',
it was a small goal -

01:18:52.933 --> 01:18:56.867
- that I should send two people out to
the churchyard in a night with lightning -

01:18:56.967 --> 01:19:02.533
- and they would dig up a corpse
to check...

01:19:04.400 --> 01:19:07.200
I'm on my way there.

01:19:07.300 --> 01:19:13.633
That's what the novel is also about, and
I think for many, to allow for something.

01:19:13.733 --> 01:19:15.867
It's not about saying something.
That's easy.

01:19:15.967 --> 01:19:20.733
But it's about creating a space
where what you say is important.

01:19:20.833 --> 01:19:26.567
I think that's important for the novel.

01:19:26.667 --> 01:19:33.767
Another starting point was:
Is it possible nowadays to write a novel -

01:19:33.867 --> 01:19:39.767
- where the Devil exists, and it works
and is something I want to read about.

01:19:39.867 --> 01:19:44.267
It's also part of my attempt to get there.

01:19:44.367 --> 01:19:51.067
- How far can you go before the reader...
- "That's not believable."

01:19:52.167 --> 01:19:58.433
My favorite Norwegian author,
Thure Erik Lund.

01:19:58.533 --> 01:20:01.667
He's fabulous.
I interviewed him once.

01:20:01.767 --> 01:20:05.633
He described his ideal novel -

01:20:05.733 --> 01:20:11.167
- as a novel where everything keeps
changing, where also the reader changes -

01:20:11.267 --> 01:20:15.933
- so that the novel in the end
might be in Chinese -

01:20:16.033 --> 01:20:19.100
- and the reader knew Chinese.

01:20:19.200 --> 01:20:21.067
That's the ideal novel.

01:20:21.167 --> 01:20:25.133
He's wild.
He's a top author.

01:20:26.933 --> 01:20:31.267
Now you'll read from 'Det tredje riket'.

01:20:31.367 --> 01:20:36.000
Before that, I'll just say that if someone
wants to buy the book and have it signed -

01:20:36.100 --> 01:20:38.667
- it can be done in the library shop
in a moment.

01:20:38.767 --> 01:20:41.600
- Now it's time for you to read.
- Yes.

01:20:42.700 --> 01:20:48.467
I'll read a little about Tove -

01:20:48.567 --> 01:20:51.400
- who started out
as a supporting character -

01:20:51.500 --> 01:20:56.933
- but took on a growing role and is now one
of the main characters in these books.

01:20:57.033 --> 01:20:58.400
She's a painter.

01:21:01.433 --> 01:21:05.400
That afternoon,
I found a perfect place in the forest.

01:21:05.500 --> 01:21:08.600
A naked, steep mountain wall
at the bottom of an incline -

01:21:08.700 --> 01:21:11.900
- that opened up onto a narrow ravine.

01:21:12.000 --> 01:21:14.433
I brought with me
a half-full bucket of red paint -

01:21:14.533 --> 01:21:17.633
- and an unopened one with white paint
from the shed -

01:21:17.733 --> 01:21:22.600
- put them together with brushes and rags
in the backpack and went on my way.

01:21:24.333 --> 01:21:27.500
The moss was easily dusted off.

01:21:27.600 --> 01:21:32.167
On the narrowest part of the ravine,
I painted him full size, in profile -

01:21:32.267 --> 01:21:33.900
- red like blood.

01:21:34.000 --> 01:21:38.000
I left the front teeming with crabs,
birds, matchstick figures -

01:21:38.100 --> 01:21:40.033
- some of them on their knees in prayer -

01:21:40.133 --> 01:21:42.567
- some with dicks sticking out
like small spears -

01:21:42.667 --> 01:21:45.067
- one of the dicks bent at an angle -

01:21:45.167 --> 01:21:48.467
- and when I painted it,
I started to laugh again.

01:21:48.567 --> 01:21:52.433
At the studio, I stuck a tube of yellow oil
paint in my pocket and was stopped by Arne -

01:21:52.533 --> 01:21:54.767
- who suddenly wanted me to prepare dinner!

01:21:54.867 --> 01:21:58.300
I broke loose and painted a yellow star
over him at the ravine -

01:21:58.400 --> 01:22:01.033
- and a yellow sun over the teeming figures.

01:22:01.133 --> 01:22:03.367
I had no idea
how long the paint would stay -

01:22:03.467 --> 01:22:07.867
- maybe some weeks, maybe months,
what did I know, maybe some years -

01:22:07.967 --> 01:22:11.600
- whatever, the plan was to come back
and carve them into the mountain -

01:22:11.700 --> 01:22:14.767
- that was easy, but not done quickly.

01:22:16.333 --> 01:22:19.267
When I no longer could see details,
and the red had turned gray -

01:22:19.367 --> 01:22:22.900
- I placed the backpack in the ravine
and went to the church.

01:22:23.000 --> 01:22:25.300
This evening the new star was to appear -

01:22:25.400 --> 01:22:31.233
- and what better place to see it
than outside the house of God?

01:22:41.767 --> 01:22:42.767
Many thanks.

01:22:43.867 --> 01:22:45.333
Many thanks.

01:22:46.800 --> 01:22:48.633
Many thanks.

01:22:49.733 --> 01:22:50.967
Thank you.

01:22:53.067 --> 01:22:54.467
Many thanks.

